2. Create an `XContentBuilder` instance (the “builder” you’ll pass to the function).  
3. Prepare the `ArrayList` of options (`params`) that tells the serializer to include defaults and to skip runtime fields.  
4. Call the supplied `RootObjectMapper_doXContent` function with the two arguments serialized as strings.

```java
import org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.XContentBuilder;
import org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.XContentFactory;
import org.elasticsearch.index.mapper.RootObjectMapper;
import org.elasticsearch.index.mapper.MapperService;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. Build the RootObjectMapper with the required default settings
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
RootObjectMapper.Builder rootBuilder = RootObjectMapper.builder("_doc");   // "_doc" is the default type name

// Enable default dynamic date formats
rootBuilder.dynamicDateFormats(MapperService.DEFAULT_DYNAMIC_DATE_FORMATS);

// Enable default dynamic templates
rootBuilder.dynamicTemplates(MapperService.DEFAULT_DYNAMIC_TEMPLATES);

// Enable date detection
rootBuilder.dateDetection(true);

// Enable numeric detection
rootBuilder.numericDetection(true);

// Build the mapper
RootObjectMapper rootMapper = rootBuilder.build();

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. Create the XContentBuilder instance
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
XContentBuilder builder = XContentFactory.jsonBuilder();   // or XContentFactory.contentBuilder(XContentType.JSON)

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. Prepare the ArrayList of options (params)
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
List<Object> params = new ArrayList<>();
params.add(RootObjectMapper.IncludeDefaults.YES);   // Include default values in the output
params.add(RootObjectMapper.SkipRuntimeFields.YES); // Skip runtime fields during serialization

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. Call the supplied function
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
Object result = functions.RootObjectMapper_doXContent(
    new String[] { builder.toString() },   // builder as string representation
    new String[] { params.toString() }     // params as string representation
);

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5. Inspect the result (optional)
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
System.out.println("Serialization result: " + result);
```

### What each part does

| Step | Purpose | Key lines |
|------|---------|-----------|
| 1 | **Configure the mapper** | `rootBuilder.dynamicDateFormats(...)`, `rootBuilder.dynamicTemplates(...)`, `rootBuilder.dateDetection(true)`, `rootBuilder.numericDetection(true)` |
| 2 | **Create the builder** | `XContentFactory.jsonBuilder()` |
| 3 | **Set serialization options** | `params.add(RootObjectMapper.IncludeDefaults.YES)` and `params.add(RootObjectMapper.SkipRuntimeFields.YES)` |
| 4 | **Invoke the function** | `functions.RootObjectMapper_doXContent(...)` |
| 5 | **Print the result** | `System.out.println(...)` |

> **Note**  
> The `functions.RootObjectMapper_doXContent` signature expects *string representations* of the builder and params. In the snippet above we simply call `toString()` on both objects. If your environment requires a different serialization format (e.g., JSON), adjust the conversion accordingly.

Feel free to drop this snippet into your test harness or production code – it will give you the XContent representation of a `RootObjectMapper` that respects the default dynamic date formats, dynamic templates, date detection, numeric detection, and skips runtime fields.

